    </div><div class="article-slot"><div class="article-section" lang="en">
    <div class="head"><h3>Twin towns and sister cities</h3></div>
    <div class="body"><p>Nairobi is <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sister_city">twinned</a> with:<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-40"
        aria-label="Note 40">40</button></sup></p><ul><li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Addis_Ababa">Addis Ababa</a>, Ethiopia<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-41"
        aria-label="Note 41">41</button></sup></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Africa">South Africa</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Town">Cape Town</a>, South Africa<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-42"
        aria-label="Note 42">42</button></sup></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States">United States</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denver">Denver</a>, United States</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/China">China</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kunming">Kunming</a>, China</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States">United States</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lowell,_Massachusetts">Lowell</a>, United States<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-43"
        aria-label="Note 43">43</button></sup></li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States">United States</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Raleigh,_North_Carolina">Raleigh</a>, United States</li>
<li><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil">Brazil</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/São_Luís,_Maranhão">São Luís</a>, Brazil<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-44"
        aria-label="Note 44">44</button></sup></li></ul></div>
</div></div></section>
            <section class="spread t-columns" id="history"
         data-spread="history"
         data-dimension="time"><h2><span class="title">history</span></h2><dl class="sheet" data-slot="sheet"><div class="row r-value"><dt class="i-calendar">inception</dt>
<dd><time datetime="1899"
      data-p="9">1899</time></dd>
<span class="tools">
        <span class="prov-none" title="—"></span>
        <a class="edit"
       href="https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3870#P571"
       aria-label="Edit"><svg aria-hidden="true" fill="currentColor" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20"><path d="m15.4.4 4.2 4.2-3.2 3.2-4.2-4.2zM10.8 5 15 9.2l-8.6 8.6-4.5 1.5-1.2-1.2 1.5-4.5z"/></svg></a></span>
</div></dl><div class="article-slot"><div class="article-section" lang="en">
    <div class="head"><h3>History</h3></div>
    <div class="body"><details class="subsection">
    <summary><h4>Early years</h4></summary><figure class="article-figure">
    <div class="images">
        <div class="cell" style="--a:1.3594">
            <a class="image" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nairobi_18_99.jpg" aria-label="Nairobi 18 99.jpg"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=960"
                 srcset="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=330 330w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=500 500w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=960 960w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=1280 1280w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%2018%2099.jpg?width=1920 1920w"
                 sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px"
                 alt=""
                 loading="lazy"></a>
        </div>
    </div><figcaption>Nairobi in 1899</figcaption>
</figure><p>The site of Nairobi was originally a swamp land occupied by a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pastoralism">pastoralist</a> people, the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maasai_people">Maasai</a>, the long-distance trader community, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kamba_people">Akamba People</a>, as well as the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Agriculturalist">agriculturalist</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kikuyu_people">Kikuyu people</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-45"
        aria-label="Note 45">45</button></sup> The name Nairobi comes from the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maasai_language">Maasai</a> expression meaning 'cool waters'. referring to the cold water stream which flowed through the area.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-46"
        aria-label="Note 46">46</button></sup> With the arrival of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uganda_Railway">Uganda Railway</a>, the site was identified by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/George_Whitehouse">Sir George Whitehouse</a> for a store depot, shunting ground and camping ground for the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indians_in_Kenya">Indian</a> labourers working on the railway.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-47"
        aria-label="Note 47">47</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-48"
        aria-label="Note 48">48</button></sup></p><p>Whitehouse, chief engineer of the railway, favoured the site as an ideal resting place due to its high elevation, temperate climate, adequate water supply and being situated before the steep ascent of the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limuru">Limuru</a> <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Escarpments">escarpments</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-47"
        aria-label="Note 47">47</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-49"
        aria-label="Note 49">49</button></sup> His choice was criticised by officials within the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/East_Africa_Protectorate">Protectorate</a> government who felt the site was too flat, poorly drained and relatively infertile.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-45"
        aria-label="Note 45">45</button></sup></p><figure class="article-figure">
    <div class="images">
        <div class="cell" style="--a:1.7638">
            <a class="image" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_entrance_to_the_Nairobi_Railway_Station_in_1899.jpg" aria-label="The entrance to the Nairobi Railway Station in 1899.jpg"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=960"
                 srcset="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=330 330w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=500 500w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=960 960w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=1280 1280w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/The%20entrance%20to%20the%20Nairobi%20Railway%20Station%20in%201899.jpg?width=1920 1920w"
                 sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px"
                 alt=""
                 loading="lazy"></a>
        </div>
    </div><figcaption>The entrance to Nairobi railway station in 1899</figcaption>
</figure><p>In the pre-colonial era, the people of modern Kenya mostly lived in villages amongst their tribes and cultural groups, where they had rulers within their communities rather than one singular government or leader.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-50"
        aria-label="Note 50">50</button></sup></p><p>In 1898, Arthur Church was commissioned to design the first town layout for the railway depot. It constituted two streets – <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tom_Mboya_Street">Victoria Street</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moi_Avenue_(Nairobi)">Station Street</a>, ten avenues, staff quarters and an Indian commercial area.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-47"
        aria-label="Note 47">47</button></sup> The railway arrived at Nairobi on 30 May 1899, and soon Nairobi replaced Machakos as the headquarters of the provincial administration for the Ukamba province.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-51"
        aria-label="Note 51">51</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-52"
        aria-label="Note 52">52</button></sup></p><p>On the arrival of the railway, Whitehouse remarked that &quot;Nairobi itself will in the course of the next two years become a large and flourishing place and already there are many applications for sites for hotels, shops and houses.&quot;<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-47"
        aria-label="Note 47">47</button></sup> The town's early years were however beset with problems of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Malaria">malaria</a> leading to at least one attempt to have the town moved.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-53"
        aria-label="Note 53">53</button></sup> In the early 1900s, Bazaar Street, now Biashara Street, was completely rebuilt after an outbreak of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bubonic_plague">plague</a> and the burning of the original town.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-54"
        aria-label="Note 54">54</button></sup></p><p>Between 1902 and 1910, the town's population rose from 5,000 to 16,000 and grew around <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Public_administration">administration</a> and tourism, initially in the form of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Big_game_hunting">big game hunting</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-55"
        aria-label="Note 55">55</button></sup> In 1907, Nairobi replaced <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mombasa">Mombasa</a> as the capital of the East Africa Protectorate.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-56"
        aria-label="Note 56">56</button></sup> In 1919, Nairobi was declared to be a municipality.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-57"
        aria-label="Note 57">57</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-58"
        aria-label="Note 58">58</button></sup></p></details><details class="subsection">
    <summary><h4>Growth</h4></summary><p>In 1921, Nairobi had 24,000 residents, of which circa 12,000 were native Africans.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-59"
        aria-label="Note 59">59</button></sup> The next decade saw growth in native African communities in Nairobi, and they began to constitute a majority for the first time.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-59"
        aria-label="Note 59">59</button></sup> This growth caused planning issues, described by Thorntorn White and his planning team as the &quot;Nairobi Problem&quot;.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-60"
        aria-label="Note 60">60</button></sup> In February 1926, colonial officer Eric Dutton passed through Nairobi on his way to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Kenya">Mount Kenya</a>, and said of the city:</p><blockquote><p>Maybe one day Nairobi will be laid out with tarred roads, with avenues of flowering trees, flanked by noble buildings; with open spaces and stately squares; a cathedral worthy of faith and country; museums and art; theatres and public offices. And it is fair to say that the Government and the Municipality have already bravely tackled the problem and that a town-plan ambitious enough to turn Nairobi into a thing of beauty has been slowly worked out, and much has already been done. But until that plan has borne fruit, Nairobi must remain what she was then, a slatternly creature, unfit to queen it over so lovely a country.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-61"
        aria-label="Note 61">61</button></sup></p>
</blockquote><p>After <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_II">World War II</a>, continuous expansion of the city angered both the indigenous <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maasai_people">Maasai</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kikuyu_people">Kikuyu</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-62"
        aria-label="Note 62">62</button></sup> This led to the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mau_Mau_Uprising">Mau Mau Uprising</a> in the 1950s, and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lancaster_House_Conferences_(Kenya)">Lancaster House Conferences</a>, which initiated a transition to Kenyan independence in 1963.</p><p>In the spring of 1950, the East African Trades Union Congress (EAUTC) led a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1950_Nairobi_general_strike">nine-day general strike</a> in the city.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-63"
        aria-label="Note 63">63</button></sup><sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-64"
        aria-label="Note 64">64</button></sup></p></details><details class="subsection">
    <summary><h4>Post independence</h4></summary><figure class="article-figure">
    <div class="images">
        <div class="cell" style="--a:1.0116">
            <a class="image" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:COLLECTIE_TROPENMUSEUM_Zicht_op_Nairobi_met_op_de_voorgrond_het_stadspark_met_tribune_TMnr_20014416.jpg" aria-label="COLLECTIE TROPENMUSEUM Zicht op Nairobi met op de voorgrond het stadspark met tribune TMnr 20014416.jpg"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=960"
                 srcset="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=330 330w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=500 500w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=960 960w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=1280 1280w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/COLLECTIE%20TROPENMUSEUM%20Zicht%20op%20Nairobi%20met%20op%20de%20voorgrond%20het%20stadspark%20met%20tribune%20TMnr%2020014416.jpg?width=1920 1920w"
                 sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px"
                 alt=""
                 loading="lazy"></a>
        </div>
    </div><figcaption>Nairobi in 1973</figcaption>
</figure><p>Nairobi remained Kenya's capital after independence, and its continued rapid growth put pressure on the city's infrastructure, with power cuts and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_scarcity">water shortages</a> becoming a common occurrence.</p><p>In September 1973, the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenyatta_International_Conference_Centre">Kenyatta International Conference Centre</a> (KICC) was opened to the public. The 28-story building at the time was designed by the Norwegian architect Karl Henrik Nøstvik and Kenyan David Mutiso. It is the only building within the city with a helipad that is open to the public. Of the buildings built in the Seventies, the KICC was the most eco-friendly and most environmentally conscious structure. Its main frame was constructed with locally available materials like gravel, sand, cement and wood, and it had wide open spaces which allowed for natural aeration and lighting. Cuboids made up the plenary hall, the tower consisted of a cylinder composed of several cuboids, and the amphitheatre and helipad both resembled cones. The tower was built around a concrete core, and it had no walls but glass windows, which allowed for maximum natural lighting. It had the largest halls in eastern and central Africa.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-65"
        aria-label="Note 65">65</button></sup></p><p>In 1972, the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_Bank">World Bank</a> approved funds for further expansion of the then Nairobi Airport (now <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jomo_Kenyatta_International_Airport">Jomo Kenyatta International Airport</a>), including a new international and domestic passenger terminal building, the airport's first dedicated cargo and freight terminal, new taxiways, associated aprons, internal roads, car parks, police and fire stations, a State Pavilion, airfield and roadway lighting, fire hydrant system, water, electrical, telecommunications and sewage systems, a dual carriageway passenger access road, security, drainage and the building of the main access road to the airport (Airport South Road). The cost of the project was more than US$29 million, US$111.8 million in 2013 dollars.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-66"
        aria-label="Note 66">66</button></sup> On 14 March 1978, construction of the terminal building was completed on the other side of the airport's single runway and opened by President <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jomo_Kenyatta">Jomo Kenyatta</a> less than five months before his death. The airport was renamed Jomo Kenyatta International Airport in memory of its first president.</p><p>The United States Embassy, then located in downtown Nairobi, was bombed in August 1998 by <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Al-Qaeda">Al-Qaeda</a> and the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egyptian_Islamic_Jihad">Egyptian Islamic Jihad</a>, as one of a series of <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998_United_States_embassy_bombings">US embassy bombings</a>. It is now the site of a memorial park.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-67"
        aria-label="Note 67">67</button></sup></p></details><details class="subsection">
    <summary><h4>21st century</h4></summary><figure class="article-figure">
    <div class="images">
        <div class="cell" style="--a:1.5059">
            <a class="image" href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nairobi_Skyline_from_BBC_Studios.jpg" aria-label="Nairobi Skyline from BBC Studios.jpg"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=960"
                 srcset="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=330 330w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=500 500w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=960 960w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=1280 1280w, https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Nairobi%20Skyline%20from%20BBC%20Studios.jpg?width=1920 1920w"
                 sizes="(max-width: 760px) 100vw, 720px"
                 alt=""
                 loading="lazy"></a>
        </div>
    </div><figcaption>Nairobi showing Fedha Towers, ICEA Building, the Nairobi Safari Club and Anniversary Towers</figcaption>
</figure><p>In November 2012, President <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mwai_Kibaki">Mwai Kibaki</a> opened the KES 31 billion Thika Superhighway. This mega-project in Kenya started in 2009 and ended in 2011. It involved expanding the four-lane carriageway to eight lanes, building underpasses, providing interchanges at roundabouts, erecting flyovers and building underpasses to ease congestion. The 50.4-kilometre road was built in three phases: Uhuru Highway to Muthaiga Roundabout; Muthaiga Roundabout to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kenyatta_University">Kenyatta University</a> and; Kenyatta University to <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thika_Town_Constituency">Thika Town</a>.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-68"
        aria-label="Note 68">68</button></sup></p><p>In May 2017, President <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uhuru_Kenyatta">Uhuru Kenyatta</a> inaugurated the Standard Gauge Railway, which connects Nairobi to Mombasa.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-69"
        aria-label="Note 69">69</button></sup> It was primarily built by a Chinese firm with about 90% of total funding from China and about 10% from the Kenyan government. A second phase is also being built, which will link <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Naivasha">Naivasha</a> to the existing route and also the <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uganda">Uganda</a> border.</p><p>In August 2020, Nairobi County Assembly Speaker <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beatrice_Elachi">Beatrice Elachi</a> resigned.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-70"
        aria-label="Note 70">70</button></sup> In December 2020, recently elected<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-71"
        aria-label="Note 71">71</button></sup> Nairobi County Assembly Speaker Benson Mutura was sworn in as acting Nairobi Governor four days after the previous Nairobi Governor <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mike_Sonko">Mike Sonko</a> was impeached and removed from office.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-72"
        aria-label="Note 72">72</button></sup> At the time of Mutura's swearing in as acting Governor, which he will hold for at least 60 days, Nairobi did not have a Deputy Governor as well.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-72"
        aria-label="Note 72">72</button></sup>
Nairobi has seen several major infrastructure projects in the 2020s. The Nairobi Expressway, completed in 2022, was developed to reduce traffic congestion along Mombasa Road.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-73"
        aria-label="Note 73">73</button></sup> In 2021, the Green Park Bus Terminal, part of efforts to improve public transport, began operations.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-74"
        aria-label="Note 74">74</button></sup> In line with the Kenyan government's Affordable Housing Program, various housing developments are underway to accommodate the city's growing population.<sup class="ref"><button type="button"
        popovertarget="note-75"
        aria-label="Note 75">75</button></sup></p></details></div>
</div></div><div class="topic-articles"><div class="topic-article">
    <div class="body">
        <p class="source"><img src="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wikipedia-logo-v2.svg?width=60" alt="" width="18"> en.wikipedia.org</p>
        <a class="title" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timeline_of_Nairobi" lang="en">Timeline of Nairobi</a></div></div></div></section>
